#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114948 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115383 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeafe81f-2d40-5413-8d19-881972161400" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Output of an Exponential Decay Function"}] :question/id #uuid "df739a77-559a-53ed-ad04-be16403b9f46" :question/math-academy-id "e-2076" :question/problem "The value of a certain stock, in dollars, decays according to the formula\n\n$V(t)= 4\\, 500\\cdot (0.85)^t$,\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the stock was bought.\n\n1. What does $V(3)$ mean in this context?\n2. Calculate $V(2) - V(3)$, and determine its meaning in this context." :question/worked-solution "- The value $V(3)$ represents the value of the function $V(t)$ at $t=3$. In this context, it represents the value of the stock $3$ years after the initial investment.\n- By similar reasoning to the above, $V(2)$ represents the stock's value $2$ years after the initial investment. Therefore, the expression\n$V(2) - V(3)$\nrepresents the total reduction in the value of the stock between $t=2$ and $t=3$ years since the initial investment.Now, let's compute its value. We get\n$V(2) - V(3)|= 4500 \\cdot (0.85)^{2} - 4500 \\cdot (0.85)^{3}; = 487.6874; \\approx 488$,\nrounded to the nearest integer. Therefore, the value of the stock dropped by $\\[math]488$ between years $2$ and $3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114949 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115384 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd0f5949-6a1a-5187-a246-8f9bac2e8d58" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting a Decay Rate"}] :question/id #uuid "d550f36c-a6f0-5b37-a998-4d44fa5f1d3d" :question/math-academy-id "e-2078" :question/problem "The market value $V(t)$ of a car, in dollars, $t$ years since it was first purchased depreciates according to the following rule:\n$V(t) = 25\\, 000\\cdot (0.95)^t$\n\nWhat does the base of the exponential expression $(0.95)$ mean in this context?" :question/worked-solution "We're asked for the decay rate, so we use the decay rate formula.\n\nThe given expression is an exponential decay expression of the form\n\n$$\ny(t) = a \\cdot (1-r)^t\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a=25\\,000$ represents the initial value of the car (in dollars).\n- $1-r = 0.95$, where $r$ is the decay rate.\n- $t$ is the time since the car was first purchased, in years.\n\nSolving for the decay rate $r$, we get\n\n$$\n1-r = 0.95\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad r = 0.05\n$$\n\nNow, $0.05$ (expressed as a percent) is equal to $5\\%$. Therefore, the value of the car depreciates by $5\\%$ each year."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114950 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115385 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab694f0f-947f-5bba-ad7a-988ea28a97fd" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Exponential Expressions"}] :question/id #uuid "795b2573-6738-5fbd-a0f4-9d94eff7aee7" :question/math-academy-id "e-2079" :question/problem "A house was purchased for $\\[math]90\\,000$. Shortly after purchasing the property, the owners discovered widening cracks in the foundation. As a result, the value of the property is reduced by one-half every year. What does the expression $\\[/math]\\,90\\,000\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^6$ mean in this context?" :question/worked-solution "The given expression is an exponential decay expression of the form\n\n$$\ny(t) = a \\cdot b^t\n$$\n\nwhere we have the following:\n\n- $a=90\\,000$ represents the purchase price of the property.\n- $b = \\dfrac 1 2$ is the decay factor.\n- $t = 6$ the time, in years, since the property was bought.\n\nSince $t=6$, the expression\n\n$$\n\\[math]\\,90\\,000\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^6\n$$\n\ngives the value of the property after $6$ years."}]}
